WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.640
Bernie Mac, Thuy Trang, Sage Stallone - these&nbsp;
actors and more were sadly all taken from us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.440
too soon. Keep watching to find out which&nbsp;
actors you may not have realized had died.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.720
One of "The Original Kings of Comedy" next&nbsp;
to Cedric the Entertainer, D. L. Hughley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.600
and Steve Harvey, Bernie Mac built his hugely&nbsp;
successful career through a stand-up routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.040
heavily influenced by Richard Pryor. That&nbsp;
in and of itself isn't unusual but Mac used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.960
that cornerstone to build a presence&nbsp;
and style that was uniquely his own.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.240
"I never watched what somebody else does. It&nbsp;
does not matter. Bernie Mac gotta do his thing."

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.040
That hard work paid off in Hollywood&nbsp;
with a series of high-profile roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.120
Many might recognize Mac as Ice Cube's neighbor,&nbsp;
Pastor Clever, in the movie "Friday." He'd go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.760
to star in "Charlie's Angels: Full Throttle,"&nbsp;
"Ocean's Eleven," "Transformers," and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.520
and also headlined a sitcom, loosely based on his&nbsp;
own experiences, called 'The Bernie Mac Show'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.760
During the last years of his life, Mac&nbsp;
suffered from Sarcoidosis, which heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.160
affected his lungs, and he succumbed to pneumonia&nbsp;
complications on August 9, 2008, at the age of 50.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:16.320
Harold Ramis was 69 when he passed away from an&nbsp;
autoimmune disease in 2014, which in and of itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.040
might have come as a surprise to many of the&nbsp;
film fans who watched him in '80s classics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.760
like "Stripes," "National Lampoon's Vacation,"&nbsp;
and "Ghostbusters." A talented director and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.560
screenwriter as well as a frequent fixture in&nbsp;
some of the biggest comedy hits of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.680
Ramis' behind-the-scenes talents helped bring&nbsp;
us "Groundhog Day" and "Caddyshack," among many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.480
others; in fact, he jumped back and forth between&nbsp;
either side of the cameras throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.200
co-writing both of the "Ghostbusters" movies&nbsp;
as well as "Stripes." Although few fans knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.760
he was ill, Ramis struggled with the side effects&nbsp;
of his infection for years—he had to learn how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.840
walk all over again after contracting autoimmune&nbsp;
inflammatory vasculitis in 2010, and suffered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.920
relapse the following year. His final onscreen&nbsp;
appearance came in the 2009 comedy Year One.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.280
A veteran of stage and screen who amassed&nbsp;
a distinguished career spanning decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.680
Richard Harris notched arguably his&nbsp;
highest-profile role late in his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.040
courtesy of the blockbuster "Harry Potter" film&nbsp;
franchise. In two of his final screen appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.000
Harris played Potter's beloved headmaster&nbsp;
Albus Dumbledore in the first two installments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.800
of the blockbuster film franchise, "The&nbsp;
Sorcerer's Stone" and "The Chamber of Secrets."

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.880
Although he'll always be Dumbledore&nbsp;
to generations of Potter-loving fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.840
outside of Hogwarts, Harris compiled multiple&nbsp;
careers' worth of impressive credits and honors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.760
He was an accomplished singer, actor, director,&nbsp;
writer, producer, and songwriter who won Grammy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.800
and Golden Globe awards for his work on 15&nbsp;
different albums and more than 70 films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.320
including "Camelot," "The Guns of&nbsp;
Navarone," "Gulliver's Travels,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.120
"Patriot Games," "Unforgiven," "Gladiator,"&nbsp;
and "The Count of Monte Cristo."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:02.160
After being diagnosed with Hodgkin's disease,&nbsp;
Harris died on October 25, 2002 at the age of 72.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.360
Best known for playing the young Carol Anne&nbsp;
from the movie "Poltergeist," Heather O'Rourke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.000
skyrocketed to instant fame as the sweet&nbsp;
young girl that terrified audiences after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.200
being sucked into the television set. In fact,&nbsp;
she may have played the part a little too well:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.600
although O'Rourke racked up an impressive list&nbsp;
of credits after her breakthrough performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.960
as Carol Anne, she remained tied to&nbsp;
the franchise in the public's mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.640
and she was one of a very small&nbsp;
number of original cast members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.600
that remained with the movie series until&nbsp;
its conclusion with 1988's "Poltergeist III."

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.760
If you've seen the final movie, you may notice&nbsp;
that Heather's cheeks appear very puffy—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.000
although audiences didn't know it at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.680
it was a reaction to the medication&nbsp;
she was taking for Crohn's disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:50.320
with which she was misdiagnosed. Tragedy struck&nbsp;
on February 1, 1988, when Heather suffered from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.440
cardiac arrest while undergoing a surgery for&nbsp;
a bowel obstruction at the tender age of 12.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.880
Thuy Trang was best known for her work as the&nbsp;
original Yellow Ranger on "Mighty Morphin Power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.480
Rangers," but unlike a lot of actors who start&nbsp;
out in genre TV, she seemed in no danger of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.920
typecast; in fact, she was developing what seemed&nbsp;
like a potentially promising career after leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.560
the show. Trang jumped right into feature work,&nbsp;
landing roles in a pair of movies released in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.960
1996: the dark drama "The Crow: City of Angels"&nbsp;
and the Leslie Neilsen comedy "Spy Hard."

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.840
Clearly, Trang had enough chops to tackle work&nbsp;
from a variety of genres, but sadly, she never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.840
had a chance to fulfill that potential—she was&nbsp;
killed in a car accident at the age of 27 in 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.680
after the driver lost control of the&nbsp;
vehicle and it plunged over a guardrail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.200
on California's Interstate 5. Emergency medical&nbsp;
personnel tried getting Trang to the hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.720
but she died in transit, the result&nbsp;
of massive internal injuries.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.200
One of Hollywood's all-time ultimate 'That&nbsp;
Guy' actors, Taylor Negron compiled a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.440
impressive list of classic screen credits&nbsp;
without ever becoming a household name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.120
Although he tended to be on screen for&nbsp;
no more than a few moments at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.600
Negron's deadpan delivery made him a&nbsp;
fan favorite among film aficionados of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.560
the '80s—and a standout in pictures like "Fast&nbsp;
Times at Ridgemont High" and "Better Off Dead".

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.760
Later in his career, Negron made memorable&nbsp;
appearances on hit TV shows like "Seinfeld"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.480
and "Friends", but as much fun as he could be&nbsp;
onscreen, he was even more interesting behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.520
the scenes. In addition to acting, Negron was an&nbsp;
acclaimed writer whose work included published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.440
collections of short stories, as well as&nbsp;
a trained painter whose work was shown in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.600
multiple galleries. He was only 57 when he died&nbsp;
in early 2015, after a battle with liver cancer.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.200
After making a name for himself&nbsp;
as a professional wrestler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.160
"Rowdy" Roddy Piper branched out onto the&nbsp;
big screen, most notably through his starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:49.840
role in the 1988 cult classic "They Live".&nbsp;
Leading that cult John Carpenter classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:55.520
Piper waged war against a covert alien invasion&nbsp;
and coined an unforgettable catchphrase.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.600
"I have come here to chew bubble gum and&nbsp;
kick ass, and I'm all out of bubble gum."

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.920
Piper's bone-crunching work in that film helped&nbsp;
set the tone for a busy Hollywood career full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.960
of B-movie roles and small-but-unforgettable&nbsp;
appearances on television shows like "It's Always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.480
Sunny in Philadelphia." On the screen as well as&nbsp;
in the ring, Piper gave every performance his all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.160
frequently elevating otherwise&nbsp;
forgettable material and consistently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.640
serving as the most memorable component&nbsp;
of whatever project he was appearing in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.800
He died in his sleep in 2015 after suffering a&nbsp;
pulmonary embolism that triggered a heart attack.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.840
Critically maligned and pretty much&nbsp;
ignored at the box office, "Rocky V"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.840
is indisputably the least loved film of Sylvester&nbsp;
Stallone's long-running boxing franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.760
Take a careful look under its cheesier&nbsp;
elements, however, and you can see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.320
outline of a truly affecting story about&nbsp;
a former heavyweight champ going broke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.240
losing his ability to box, and nearly&nbsp;
losing his relationship with his son.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.000
To help tell the story, Stallone&nbsp;
brought in his own 14-year-old son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.600
Sage, to play the part of Robert&nbsp;
"Rocky Jr." Balboa in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.320
and the young Stallone's time on the set clearly&nbsp;
made a positive impression: after college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.200
Sage continued working in the industry in small,&nbsp;
low-key projects, carving out a career in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.480
own right apart from his blockbuster-leading&nbsp;
father. Unfortunately, Sage died on July 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.840
2012, at the young age of 36 due to a heart&nbsp;
attack brought on by coronary artery disease.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.120
Long before he founded an ill-fated&nbsp;
amusement park using the secrets of dino DNA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.760
Richard Attenborough was already an&nbsp;
accomplished and award-winning actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.880
Attenborough starred alongside Steve McQueen in&nbsp;
"The Great Escape", released in the midst of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.640
busy period that included some of his most widely&nbsp;
seen work. He also won two consecutive Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:51.040
Globe Awards in 1967 and 1968 for co-starring&nbsp;
in "The Sand Pebbles" and "Doctor Dolittle".

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.920
Needless to say, Attenborough's career contains&nbsp;
no shortage of highlights, and his work will no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.120
doubt be familiar to classic film fans. Younger&nbsp;
filmgoers, however, have their own Attenborough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.600
memories thanks to his appearance as John Hammond,&nbsp;
whose ambitions led to the creation of Jurassic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.400
Park. That blockbuster made him famous to a whole&nbsp;
new generation, but it's just one short chapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.560
in a life of many great cinematic achievements&nbsp;
that ended on August 24, 2014 at the age of 90.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:18.640
"Whatchoo talkin' 'bout Willis?"

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:23.920
Kids of the '70s and '80s and honestly&nbsp;
anyone who enjoys the classic sitcoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.560
of the era will immediately recognize the&nbsp;
immortal catchphrase Gary Coleman coined as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.560
an adorable kid on the long-running&nbsp;
hit sitcom "Diff'rent Strokes."

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.800
Coleman's diminutive stature helped him continue&nbsp;
to play younger characters throughout much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.920
his career, and at the peak of his fame, he&nbsp;
landed a series of film and television roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.400
Unfortunately, his youthful looks&nbsp;
and peak height of 4 feet 8 inches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.040
were side effects of an autoimmune kidney disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.560
combined with the medications he took&nbsp;
throughout his life to fight the condition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.320
Work in Hollywood became harder to come by after&nbsp;
"Strokes" left the airwaves, and behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.520
scenes, he underwent frequent dialysis treatments,&nbsp;
and was forced to undergo two kidney transplants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:11.120
After a fall at his home, Coleman died on May 28,&nbsp;
2010 of an epidural hematoma at the age of 42.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.560
Exactly where you remember esteemed actor&nbsp;
Edward Herrmann from depends on whether you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.840
favor musicals, vampire movies, medical TV shows,&nbsp;
or fast-talking mother-daughter family dramas.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:28.160
Herrmann is probably best recognized today for&nbsp;
his role as pompous but warm-hearted patriarch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.520
Richard Gilmore in the series "Gilmore Girls."&nbsp;
Richard's dry sense of humor and quiet authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.320
contrasted with the quick wit and constant&nbsp;
banter that flew between his daughter Lorelai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.880
his granddaughter Rory and his wife&nbsp;
Emily, who was played by Kelly Bishop.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.120
Offscreen, Herrmann and Bishop, both classically&nbsp;
trained and meticulous about their craft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:52.720
were especially close. Herrmann's wife Star, whom&nbsp;
he married in 1994, would joke that Bishop was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:57.840
his other wife. In Herrmann's final moments, his&nbsp;
family invited Bishop to say goodbye with them.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.720
Before his longest-running role in "Gilmore&nbsp;
Girls," Herrmann worked regularly on TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:06.640
and in movies. He was nominated for&nbsp;
an Emmy for playing a priest in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.960
medical drama "St. Elsewhere," and won&nbsp;
one for the legal series "The Practice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.200
He played Franklin D. Roosevelt in the 1982&nbsp;
adaptation of "Annie" — having previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.320
played the President for a miniseries and&nbsp;
TV movie in the late '70s. And in 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.240
he played a stepfather from literal Hell&nbsp;
in the vampire movie "The Lost Boys."

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.880
Herrmann died of brain cancer,&nbsp;
at age 71 on December 31,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.280
2014. In the Netflix revival of&nbsp;
"Gilmore Girls," it was revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.200
that Richard had also died, sparking&nbsp;
big changes for Emily in particular.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:46.480
Deezer D was one of the foundational actors on&nbsp;
hit medical drama "ER." Born Dearon Thompson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:53.440
he played nurse Malik McGrath for the show's&nbsp;
entire 15-year run, appearing in 188 episodes.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:57.840
As often happens in real hospitals as well&nbsp;
as fictional ones, "ER" typically focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.640
on its physicians and surgeons, not the nurses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.040
But Thompson was one of just a handful&nbsp;
of actors who appeared in every season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.200
and his episode count makes him the fifth&nbsp;
most prolific member of the large cast.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:15.040
Outside of "ER," Thompson had a few small parts in&nbsp;
movies including the cult classic comedy "Romy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.040
Michele's High School Reunion" and appeared&nbsp;
in Vanilla Ice's video for "Cool as Ice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.120
He played one of the members of a fictional rap&nbsp;
group in the mockumentary movie "CB4" in 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:30.320
and also rapped for real. He released multiple&nbsp;
records, including the full-length album "Delayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.520
But Not Denied" in 2008, and continued making&nbsp;
songs until his death. His final track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.400
"History Can't Be Stopped,"&nbsp;
was released posthumously.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:43.440
Thompson started having heart issues around 2008,&nbsp;
telling RadarOnline the following year that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.840
had been suffering from recurrent pneumonia&nbsp;
and heart failure. It was discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:53.440
he had a leaking valve and expanded aorta,&nbsp;
which was addressed with surgery in 2009.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:57.760
According to Deadline, he went on to have more&nbsp;
operations on his heart over the next 10 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:04.160
or so. Thompson died on January 7, 2021, at&nbsp;
the age of 55, of a suspected heart attack.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.200
Kathryn Joosten was in her 50s when&nbsp;
she started working consistently in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:13.040
notoriously youth-centric&nbsp;
Hollywood in the mid 1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.680
She'd started acting in 1980, after realizing&nbsp;
she was missing out on following her dreams.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:23.280
Despite her late start, Joosten built a career&nbsp;
playing one-off but always memorable characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.560
in hit TV series. Her credits include "ER," "3rd&nbsp;
Rock From the Sun," "Seinfeld,'' "Roseanne,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.200
"The Nanny," "Frasier," "Buffy the Vampire&nbsp;
Slayer," and "The X Files," to name just a few.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.880
Later she would add the likes of "Will &amp;&nbsp;
Grace" and "Grey's Anatomy" to that list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:41.920
In short, name a hit show of the '90s and&nbsp;
'00s, and Joosten probably appeared in it.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:46.480
Her most memorable guest appearance was as a&nbsp;
woman who refused treatment for kidney failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:51.600
in Season 1 of "Scrubs." The episode, "My Old&nbsp;
Lady," consistently ranks as a fan favorite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:54.320
thanks in no small part to Joosten's performance.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.000
"Unfortunately we don't really have a choice."

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.080
"Actually I do have a choice."

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:02.000
"Certain things you never expect&nbsp;
to come out of a person's mouth."

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.080
"I think I'm ready to die."

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:09.280
In addition to those guest roles, Joosten&nbsp;
landed regular parts in two major series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.640
She played the steely secretary to&nbsp;
Martin Sheen's President Bartlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:17.600
in two seasons of "The West Wing," combining&nbsp;
her talents for fast-paced drama and comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.560
and nosy but occasionally helpful neighbor&nbsp;
Karen McCluskey in "Desperate Housewives."

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.600
Joosten was first treated for lung cancer&nbsp;
in 2001, the year "My Old Lady" aired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.520
She was undergoing treatment while making the&nbsp;
final season of "Desperate Housewives," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:36.000
was written into her character's arc.&nbsp;
McCluskey died in the series finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.840
Joosten died two weeks after that episode&nbsp;
aired, on June 2, 2012, at the age of 72.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:48.720
Hugh Dane's resume reads like a list of the top&nbsp;
sitcoms of the '90s and early '00s. He played two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.320
different characters in "The Fresh Prince of&nbsp;
Bel-Air," appeared in an episode of "Martin,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:57.760
and multiple episodes of "Roc." He had small parts&nbsp;
in "Friends," "Sister, Sister," "Everybody Hates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:03.440
Chris," and "New Girl." On the drama side, he&nbsp;
played a judge in an episode of "The West Wing."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:07.440
He also appeared briefly in the comedy&nbsp;
movies "Little Fockers" and "Bridesmaids."

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.840
However, Dane will most likely be&nbsp;
remembered and missed for his role as Hank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:15.280
the permanently unimpressed&nbsp;
security guard in "The Office."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.040
He appeared in 22 episodes of NBC's&nbsp;
hit mockumentary about the inner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:23.440
workings and ridiculous shenanigans in&nbsp;
a Scranton, Pennsylvania paper company.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:31.680
Dane died at the age of 75 on May 16, 2018.&nbsp;
After his death, his "The Office" co-star Rainn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:37.680
Wilson described him as "one of the greats.&nbsp;
So kind, funny, talented." On January 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:43.600
2021, NBC's streaming platform Peacock tweeted&nbsp;
a video of a cold open that never aired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:48.480
dedicated to Dane. It showed one of Jim's&nbsp;
most elaborate pranks on nemesis Dwight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.960
featuring Dane as Morpheus from "The Matrix."

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.400
"If the blue pill's not safe to&nbsp;
swallow, someone should tell us now!"

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.160
Kenny Baker originated one of the most beloved&nbsp;
characters in one of the most popular movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:05.440
franchises of all time — but you wouldn't&nbsp;
recognize him from that role out of costume.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:10.000
Baker is most famous for playing R2-D2, the&nbsp;
brave and mischievous "Star Wars" droid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.960
He was born with Dwarfism, but it wasn't just&nbsp;
Baker's height that made him perfect for the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:18.320
According to his New York Times&nbsp;
obituary, he was the only person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:22.400
small enough to fit into the costume&nbsp;
who was also strong enough to move it.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:28.240
It helped that Baker had been in entertainment&nbsp;
for years: He sang, danced, played instruments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:33.120
clowned, and ice skated in various touring&nbsp;
companies. As he told the Guardian -

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:37.120
"There are certain closeup movements&nbsp;
only I can do convincingly. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:41.280
all about giving R2 a personality,&nbsp;
and CGI can never really do that."

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:46.080
In addition to R2, Baker played an Ewok&nbsp;
in "Return of the Jedi," alongside his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.240
wife and fellow actor Eileen. But the&nbsp;
furry, violent inhabitants of the jungle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.800
planet Endor were less fun to play than&nbsp;
to watch, thanks to the stuffy costumes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:59.360
It wasn't just that they were hot: The heads were&nbsp;
impossible to see out of when they steamed up.

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:05.200
The last time Baker played R2 was for "Revenge&nbsp;
of the Sith," although he served as a consultant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.600
on "The Force Awakens." He also appeared&nbsp;
in many other movies and series, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:16.480
"Flash Gordon," "Time Bandits" and "Amadeus."&nbsp;
He died at the age of 81 on August 13, 2016.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.600
If you're a millennial who grew up wishing you&nbsp;
could have an identical twin to swap lives with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:26.320
you probably remember Natasha Richardson&nbsp;
best for her role as the extremely cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:30.480
fashion designer mom in the Lindsay&nbsp;
Lohan-led remake of "The Parent Trap."

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:34.880
That was Richardson's most prominent screen&nbsp;
role, but she had a prestigious stage career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:39.680
It started with a touring version of "The&nbsp;
Seagull" which ended in London's West End,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:44.480
with Richardson appearing opposite her mother,&nbsp;
renowned British actor Vanessa Redgrave.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:49.120
TV fans may know Vanessa best for her&nbsp;
role as Dr. Erica Noughton in "Nip/Tuck,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:55.120
opposite her other daughter and Natasha's sister,&nbsp;
Joely Richardson. Joely should also be known to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:01.440
fans from "The Parent Trap" era as Anita in the&nbsp;
1996 live-action adaptation of "101 Dalmatians."

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.440
As you've probably noted, Natasha&nbsp;
was part of an acting dynasty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:10.000
Her grandfather Sir Michael Redgrave,&nbsp;
Vanessa's father, was famous for tragedies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:15.280
Natasha's aunt, Lynn Redgrave, Vanessa's sister,&nbsp;
was an Oscar-nominated theater superstar. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:20.160
round out the family, Lynn and Vanessa's brother,&nbsp;
Corin Redgrave, was a stage actor and playwright.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:23.360
Natasha met actor Liam Neeson&nbsp;
when they starred opposite each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.080
other in a Broadway production of "Anna Christie."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:30.640
She was nominated for a Tony for the role, and&nbsp;
the two later married and had two sons together.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:35.920
Natasha died suddenly of head injuries&nbsp;
sustained during a skiing trip on March 18,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.400
2009. She was 45.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:42.720
In a show that made superstars&nbsp;
out of its six main cast members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:47.920
James Michael Tyler managed to hold his own&nbsp;
as one of the most memorable side characters.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:53.120
He played coffee shop manager Gunther in&nbsp;
"Friends" for all 10 seasons, appearing in 150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:58.720
episodes total, although he didn't have a line&nbsp;
for his first 33 appearances. Tyler originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:03.360
landed the role because he knew how to work the&nbsp;
fancy espresso machine on the Central Perk set.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:07.280
Not one to take himself overly seriously,&nbsp;
after "Friends," he played himself in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:11.520
satire "Episodes," which saw that version&nbsp;
of Tyler experiencing a slightly awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:16.160
reunion with Matt LeBlanc, who played&nbsp;
Joey on "Friends," also playing himself.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.320
Tyler loved the experience of being in&nbsp;
"Friends," although there was one drawback.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:25.040
As he told Digital Spy, he was cast with&nbsp;
very distinctive bleached blonde hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:29.360
which added to his character's mystique. But&nbsp;
it was originally the result of an experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:33.920
he let one of his friends perform, which&nbsp;
turned into weekly bleach jobs for 10 years.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:42.160
Tyler was diagnosed with prostate cancer in 2018,&nbsp;
which he didn't make public until June 2021. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:46.800
month, he announced in an interview with NBC that&nbsp;
the cancer had reached stage four, meaning it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:53.280
had spread to other parts of his body. He died&nbsp;
just a few months later, on October 24, 2021.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:59.280
A veteran stage, film, and television actor,&nbsp;
Richard Griffiths is known by the masses for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:05.200
portrayal of Harry Potter's uncle, Vernon Dursley.&nbsp;
While Dursley was for the most part always cruel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:09.600
to the young wizard, he was still a part of&nbsp;
Harry's family—and he ultimately helped prove that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:15.200
the Boy Who Lived had a golden heart because he&nbsp;
still cared for the uncle who treated him poorly.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:20.240
Outside of the "Harry Potter" series, Griffiths&nbsp;
won a slew of awards over the course of a busy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:24.000
career, earning particular acclaim for&nbsp;
his portrayal of English teacher Douglas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:29.040
Hector in a play called "The History Boys."&nbsp;
He also starred in a variety of projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:33.360
including "Superman II," "Gandhi," "Sleepy&nbsp;
Hollow," "King Ralph," "Hugo," "Pirates of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:38.080
the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides," and "The&nbsp;
Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy." Griffiths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:44.480
passed away at 65 on March 28, 2013 due to&nbsp;
complications shortly after having heart surgery.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:50.000
Brad Renfro skyrocketed to fame at&nbsp;
age 11 through his acclaimed work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:54.320
Joel Schumacher's adaptation of author&nbsp;
John Grisham's book "The Client."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:59.920
High profile roles quickly followed, and Renfro&nbsp;
made appearances in "Sleepers" opposite Brad Pitt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:05.040
"The Informers" starring Kim Basinger, and&nbsp;
"Ghost World" alongside Scarlett Johansson.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:10.320
Yet as his star continued to rise on screen,&nbsp;
Renfro faced a darker path behind the scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:15.520
one that would ultimately see him fulfilling the&nbsp;
sad destiny of many child actors, plunging into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:20.720
life of illegal drug use and trouble with the law.&nbsp;
After attempting to buy drugs from an undercover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:25.840
L.A. police officer, Renfro pleaded no contest to&nbsp;
attempted possession of heroin and spent ten days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:32.400
in jail. On January 15, 2008, at 25 years old,&nbsp;
Renfro's turbulent story reached a tragic end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:37.600
when he was found dead of an accidental heroin and&nbsp;
morphine overdose in his apartment in Los Angeles.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:42.720
Dana Plato played Kimberly Drummond in&nbsp;
the long-running hit television series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:47.520
Diff'rent Strokes. Cast alongside fellow&nbsp;
child stars Gary Coleman and Todd Bridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:52.480
she was catapulted to instant stardom. To&nbsp;
cope with the mounting pressures of fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:58.080
Plato often indulged in cocaine and marijuana&nbsp;
off the set—yet it wasn't until she became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.640
pregnant that the network released her from&nbsp;
her contract, helping her trigger a downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:07.760
spiral of drugs and alcohol that included work&nbsp;
in adult films and a spread in Playboy magazine.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:13.520
She later moved to Las Vegas, where, desperate&nbsp;
for cash, she robbed a store for less than $200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:20.080
and was bonded out of jail by Vegas entertainer&nbsp;
Wayne Newton. She died at the age of 34 on May 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:25.120
1999, in her RV in Oklahoma after taking&nbsp;
a fatal dose of lortab and valium.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:30.960
Best known for her work starring as Mel Gibson's&nbsp;
daughter in the blockbuster "The Patriot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:35.200
Skye McCole Bartusiak was a child&nbsp;
actress with a promising future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:39.120
She made her film debut in "The Cider&nbsp;
House Rules" in 1999 and starred with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:43.440
Michael Douglas and Brittany Murphy in&nbsp;
"Don't Say a Word" in 2001. She also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:49.120
appeared in the film "Kill your Darlings" with&nbsp;
"True Blood" star Alexander Skarsgard in 2006.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:54.240
Unfortunately, Bartusiak's life behind the&nbsp;
scenes wasn't as glamorous as fans might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:59.280
assumed—and included some dangerous drug habits&nbsp;
that would end up contributing to her having an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:04.480
accidental overdose in 2014. According to&nbsp;
Bartusiak's mother, the young star had also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:09.600
been suffering from epileptic seizures prior to&nbsp;
her passing, which led the family to initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:13.760
believe she'd choked to death before the&nbsp;
coroner's ruling made the tragic truth clear.

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.200
Before getting his big break, Michael K.&nbsp;
Williams was consistently cast as one-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:23.920
criminals. He was a young Black man in a white&nbsp;
industry that didn't know how to tell diverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:28.880
stories about people who looked like him. Also,&nbsp;
he had a distinctive facial scar from the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.400
someone attacked him with a razor–something&nbsp;
else Hollywood didn't know what to do with.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:37.040
Williams' big break came courtesy&nbsp;
of "The Wire" character Omar Little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:41.440
an armed robber who targets drug dealers,&nbsp;
and the most feared man in a territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:46.320
ruled by violence. But Little wasn't a&nbsp;
carbon copy of those previous characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:51.840
For one thing, he was openly gay, in a homophobic&nbsp;
society that mocked gay men as weak. For another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.240
he was known to give some of his ill-gotten&nbsp;
gains to people in his much-in-need community.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:00.640
After playing Omar, Williams landed&nbsp;
roles in several prestigious series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:05.600
including crime drama "The Night Of," "When&nbsp;
We Rise," about the gay civil rights movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:09.920
and a recurring part as a racketeer in&nbsp;
Prohibition drama "Boardwalk Empire."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:15.200
He even got to show off his comedy skills&nbsp;
in "Community." In 2019, he appeared in Ava&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:20.640
DuVernay's "When They See Us," as the father of&nbsp;
one of the wrongly accused Central Park Five.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:26.960
In 2020, he was nominated for his fifth&nbsp;
Emmy for the HBO series "Lovecraft Country."

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:31.040
Williams died at the age&nbsp;
of 54 on September 6, 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:33.600
of what was later confirmed as a drug overdose.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:38.080
If you or anyone you know is&nbsp;
struggling with addiction issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:42.800
help is available. Visit the Substance Abuse&nbsp;
and Mental Health Services Administration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:50.089
website or contact SAMHSA's National&nbsp;
Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP (4357).

